<sn><md>#### How Regex Solves "Dynamic" Pattern Matching vs. Plain `find()`

- A **plain find** (Ctrl+F, `str.find()`, `indexOf`) answers exactly one question:
 "Where does this `exact sequence` of characters appear?"*

- A **regex find** answers:
 *"Where does `any string of this shape` appear?"*

The word "dynamic" is the key. Plain find is **static** — the needle is fixed at the moment you type it. Regex is **dynamic** — the needle is a *rule* that describes a whole family of possible strings.

</md></sn><sn><md>#### Plain Find: One Literal, One Match

```js
"the cat sat on the mat".indexOf("cat")   // 4
"the cat sat on the mat".indexOf("c.t")   // -1  ← no literal "c.t"
```

Browser Ctrl+F and most editor finds do this (with options for case, whole word). They're fast because they use simple algorithms (Boyer-Moore, etc.) over a **fixed string**.

The limitation is obvious: the needle must be **exactly** what you typed. To find a date, you'd have to type every possible date.
</md></sn><sn><md>#### Regex: A Rule That Generates Matches

Regex replaces the literal needle with a **pattern language**:

```js
/\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}/        // any YYYY-MM-DD
/colou?r/                  // color or colour
/\b\w+@\w+\.\w+\b/         // any simple email
/"[^"]*"/                  // any double-quoted string
```

Now the "needle" isn't a string — it's a **set of strings**. The matcher walks the input and, at each position, asks *"can the pattern start here and succeed?"*

That's the dynamism: **the pattern is computed at match time against the input**, not fixed in advance.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Where the Dynamism Actually Comes From

Regex engines add four things plain find can't express:

- a) Character classes — "any of these"

	```
	[aeiou]     any vowel
	[^0-9]      any non-digit
	\d \w \s    digit, word char, whitespace
	.           any char (except newline by default)
	```

	Plain find can't say "any digit." It can only say "the digit 7."

- b) Quantifiers — "how many"

	```
	a*      zero or more
	a+      one or more
	a?      zero or one
	a{2,5}  between 2 and 5
	```

	Plain find can't express "one or more digits." You'd need a loop.

- c) Alternation — "one of these branches"

	```
	cat|dog|fish
	(jpg|jpeg|png|gif)
	```

	Plain find can only hold one literal at a time (though editors let you search multiple terms — that's a different mechanism, not one pattern).

- d) Anchors &amp; boundaries — "in this position"

	```
	^   start of line
	$   end of line
	\b  word boundary
	```

	This lets the pattern depend on **where** it appears, not just what it is.

	Together these make the pattern **compositional** — small rules combine into a rule that describes an unbounded set of strings.
</md></sn><sn><md>### The Matching Process (Why It's "Dynamic")

A regex engine, at each position in the input, runs a tiny state machine:

```
Input:  "order 1234 shipped 2026-10-09"
Pattern: /\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}/

Pos 0: 'o' — can \d start? no → advance
Pos 1: 'r' — no → advance
...
Pos 17: '2' — \d matches → try \d\d\d then '-' ...
        succeeds → match "2026-10-09"
```

The engine **doesn't know in advance** where the match is or what it will contain. It discovers it by consuming input character by character, backtracking (in backtracking engines) or tracking states (in NFA/DFA engines).

That's the dynamic part: **the match is constructed at runtime from the input, guided by the pattern.**
</md></sn><sn><md>### Capture Groups: Extracting the Dynamic Parts

This is where regex leaps past find for real work. Groups let you **pull out the varying pieces**:

```js
const m = "2026-10-09".match(/(\d{4})-(\d{2})-(\d{2})/);
// m[1] = "2026"   m[2] = "10"   m[3] = "09"
```

Plain find gives you a position. Regex gives you a **structured extraction** — the pattern defines both *what to find* and *what to keep*.

Named groups make it readable:

```js
/(?<year>\d{4})-(?<month>\d{2})-(?<day>\d{2})/
```
</day></month></year></md></sn><sn><md>### Why Editors/Browsers Add Regex Mode

Plain find is optimized for the 90% case: "find this word." It's fast, simple, and uses string algorithms that don't need a state machine.

But users constantly need:

| Need | Plain find | Regex |
|---|---|---|
| Find any number | ✗ | `\d+` |
| Find any email | ✗ | `\S+@\S+\.\S+` |
| Find lines starting with `#` | ✗ | `^#.*` |
| Find `color` or `colour` | two searches | `colou?r` |
| Find `foo` not followed by `bar` | ✗ | `foo(?!bar)` |
| Replace with captured parts | ✗ | `$1`, `$2` |

So editors expose regex as an **opt-in mode** — you pay the complexity cost only when the literal-search model can't express what you want.
</md></sn><sn><md>### Regex vs. Plain Find: The Trade-off

| | Plain find | Regex |
|---|---|---|
| Needle | Fixed literal | Rule / pattern |
| Speed | Very fast (Boyer-Moore etc.) | Slower; can backtrack |
| Expressiveness | One string | Whole family of strings |
| Extraction | Position only | Captures / groups |
| Debuggability | Trivial | Can be hard |
| Risk | None | ReDoS, catastrophic backtracking |

**Rule of thumb:** if you can type the exact string, use plain find. If you'd otherwise need a loop, a second pass, or "any X where Y," use regex.
</md></sn><sn><md>### A Concrete Side-by-Side

**Goal:** find all ISO dates in a log file.

Plain find approach (JS):
```js
// You'd need to know every date in advance — impossible.
// So you write a loop:
let i = 0, found = [];
while (i &lt; text.length) {
  if (/\d/.test(text[i])) {
    // manually check 4 digits, dash, 2 digits, dash, 2 digits...
    // you are effectively re-implementing a regex by hand
  }
  i++;
}
```

Regex approach:
```js
const dates = text.match(/\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}/g);
// ["2026-10-09", "2025-01-01", ...]
```

The regex **is** the loop, the digit check, the dash check, and the length check — compressed into a declarative rule. That's the whole point: regex lets you *describe the shape* instead of *programming the search*.
</md></sn><sn><md>### The Deep Reason It Works

Plain find matches against **one string**.
Regex matches against a **language** — a (usually) regular set of strings.

- A finite automaton has a fixed number of states
- It consumes input one character at a time
- At each step, the state encodes "what part of the pattern am I in?"
- A match is simply: start in the start state, end in an accepting state

Because the automaton is finite, it can match **any string in the language**, no matter how long — without knowing the string in advance. That's the dynamism: **the pattern is a machine, and the machine runs on whatever input you feed it.**
</md></sn><sn><md>### Summary

| Question | Plain find | Regex |
|---|---|---|
| What is the needle? | A fixed string | A rule (a regular language) |
| When is it decided? | At search time, by you | At pattern-write time, as a shape |
| What varies? | Nothing | Everything the pattern allows |
| Can it extract? | Position only | Yes, via groups |
| Why "dynamic"? | It isn't | The match is *constructed* from input |

Regex solves dynamic pattern matching by **replacing the literal needle with a small program (a finite automaton) that describes a whole set of needles** — and then running that program against the input at match time. Plain find can only ever look for the one string you typed; regex looks for the *shape* of the string.</md></sn>